WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:04.710
Let's get right into audience questions for
the week, one that is increasingly coming

00:00:04.710 --> 00:00:06.200
in in different forms.

00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:11.200
Is would it really be possible for the United
States to vote by mail in the 2020 presidential

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:12.440
election?

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:17.390
I think one thing that's important is you've
got to reframe this question is what it actually

00:00:17.390 --> 00:00:24.630
is, which is would all 50 states really be
able to vote by mail in the 2020 election

00:00:24.630 --> 00:00:31.119
because you really have 50 state elections
in the 2020 presidential election.

00:00:31.119 --> 00:00:37.660
So while the debate has been, Nancy Pelosi
says she wants vote by mail in November.

00:00:37.660 --> 00:00:38.660
Trump doesn't.

00:00:38.660 --> 00:00:39.910
And we know why Trump doesn't.

00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:43.530
It would be bad for Trump for more people
to more easily be able to vote.

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:48.479
Trump, in fact, is essentially counting on
his supporters going out to vote.

00:00:48.479 --> 00:00:54.479
But Biden supporters not going to vote maybe
because of fears of the Corona virus.

00:00:54.479 --> 00:00:57.820
We don't exactly know where that's going to
be yet in November.

00:00:57.820 --> 00:01:01.659
But for a lot of states, though, some states
already have vote by mail, states that don't.

00:01:01.659 --> 00:01:05.489
What you're really talking about, you know,
they the right loves to make it seem like

00:01:05.489 --> 00:01:07.280
this is this overwhelming task.

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.460
How do we go from no vote by mail to national
vote by mail?

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.320
Well, if you already have absentee ballots,
which as far as I know, every state or almost

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.070
every state does, you have to scale up the
absentee ballot program.

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:23.689
You've got to print more ballots in advance.

00:01:23.689 --> 00:01:30.249
In some states, there are laws that say you
can't just send an absentee ballot to everyone.

00:01:30.249 --> 00:01:34.429
People have to request it and then you are
able to send it to them.

00:01:34.429 --> 00:01:40.060
I've argued that simply pointing at the policy
is not really proactive governance.

00:01:40.060 --> 00:01:46.770
Instead, we should say let's pass something
that this year, because of extenuating circumstances,

00:01:46.770 --> 00:01:49.289
you don't have to request an absentee ballot.

00:01:49.289 --> 00:01:51.540
One will merely be sent to you.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:57.179
And then it's just you know, we're talking
about a mass mail campaign, essentially.

00:01:57.179 --> 00:02:02.889
So the issue of getting the ballots to people
could be solved if we start dealing with it

00:02:02.889 --> 00:02:03.889
now.

00:02:03.889 --> 00:02:07.950
The issue of printing enough ballots could
be solved if we start dealing with it now.

00:02:07.950 --> 00:02:15.799
The economic cost of the postage is far smaller
than the economic cost of the election, potentially

00:02:15.799 --> 00:02:20.400
escalating or causing a second wave of corona
virus infection.

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:22.480
And we've got about seven months to do it.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:23.989
So the answer is yes.

00:02:23.989 --> 00:02:27.230
The U.S. really could vote by mail in 2020.

00:02:27.230 --> 00:02:29.680
But you would need to start that process now.

00:02:29.680 --> 00:02:36.799
And it seems to me that especially appearing
to be past the peak on this first wave of

00:02:36.799 --> 00:02:43.340
Corona virus, the political appetite for doing
that, especially from the right, is not going

00:02:43.340 --> 00:02:45.170
to be enough to reach critical mass.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:52.670
Now, sadly, if a second wave gets going closer
to the fall, you then may get a lot more people,

00:02:52.670 --> 00:02:55.780
even Republicans, saying we've got to do vote
by mail.

00:02:55.780 --> 00:03:00.480
We have to expand absentee ballot to everybody
in states that have absentee ballots instead

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:01.760
of vote by mail.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:04.340
It probably will be too late by then.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:06.400
That's the unfortunate reality.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:12.480
By that point, the process of printing all
of the ballots, putting in place enough a

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:17.050
sort of public information about the fact
that this is the way it's being done, dealing

00:03:17.050 --> 00:03:20.210
with the fact that many states have a rule
that you can only send an absentee ballot

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:21.491
to someone who has requested it.

00:03:21.491 --> 00:03:23.239
You can't just start sending them out by then.

00:03:23.239 --> 00:03:24.239
It will be too late.

00:03:24.239 --> 00:03:30.000
Like if we don't deal with this, if on September
25th, we see that wave, two of corona viruses

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:34.269
exploding and we've got to deal with this,
it probably will be too late by then.

00:03:34.269 --> 00:03:37.590
The question mark right now, the test case
is Ohio.

00:03:37.590 --> 00:03:41.720
Ohio, you might remember, was scheduled to
have their primary in March.

00:03:41.720 --> 00:03:45.710
They postponed it because of the virus, said
it would be done June 2nd have since changed

00:03:45.710 --> 00:03:51.780
and said we're going to run sort of like a
nearly month long vote by mail campaign, but

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:52.980
until April 20th.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:58.500
But everybody has to request a ballot again,
relying on this thing about we can't just

00:03:58.500 --> 00:04:00.549
send the ballots, people have to request them.

00:04:00.549 --> 00:04:05.620
So we will see in Ohio how it works in the
Democratic primary and then we'll see if there's

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:08.629
the political will from states to actually
do this now.

00:04:08.629 --> 00:04:12.780
What you have to also remember is because
these are state decisions, you could have

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:18.050
a situation in November where the states that
are likely to be Biden states do the vote

00:04:18.050 --> 00:04:20.790
by mail and Biden wins regardless.

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:25.400
And the states that are likely to be Trump
anyway don't setup vote by mail and they go

00:04:25.400 --> 00:04:26.400
to Trump.

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:31.670
The difference maker, as usual, will be what
is Michigan do?

00:04:31.670 --> 00:04:33.810
What does Pennsylvania do?

00:04:33.810 --> 00:04:35.000
What does North Carolina do?

00:04:35.000 --> 00:04:37.980
Those few states that end up making a difference.

00:04:37.980 --> 00:04:39.310
And then it's anyone's guess.

00:04:39.310 --> 00:04:43.400
This is one area where it's good that Michigan
has a Democratic governor.

00:04:43.400 --> 00:04:46.940
It's good that Kentucky now has a Democratic
governor, because it could be the difference

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:47.960
maker in.

00:04:47.960 --> 00:04:50.470
Do they vote by mail or not in November?

00:04:50.470 --> 00:04:56.350
And thus does the election flip towards Joe
Biden or back towards a Donald Trump reelection?

00:04:56.350 --> 00:04:57.350
Scary stuff.

00:04:57.350 --> 00:04:57.355
A lot riding on it.

